%I A402127 #6 Nov 18 2026 20:54:12
%S A402127 1,2,3,4,5,5,6,6,7,7,7,7,8,7,7,7,8
%N A402127 Least number of Wang tile types that tile an n X n square but not an (n+1) X (n+1) square.
%C A402127 A Wang tile is a unit square with a color on each of its four edges, and a tile type is given by its north, east, south and west colors. From a set of types, each type may be used any number of times, but tiles may not be rotated or reflected. A square is tiled if its cells are filled with tiles so that any two tiles that share an edge have the same color on that edge; there is no condition on the edges along the boundary of the square. A set of types that tiles the n X n square but not the (n+1) X (n+1) square is said to stop at n (it then tiles no larger square, since a larger tiled square would contain a tiled (n+1) X (n+1) square). a(n) is the least number of types in a set that stops at n.
%C A402127 The sequence is not monotone: a(13) = 8, but a(14) = a(15) = a(16) = 7. For each of n = 12, 14, 15 and 16 there is a set of seven types that stops at n, but no set of at most seven types stops at 13. For 1 <= n <= 11, a(n) = A327705(n), where A327705(n) is the minimum number of parts in a partition of n whose parts can be merged to obtain every partition of n into at most five parts; the two sequences differ at n = 12, where a(12) = 7 and A327705(12) = 8.
%C A402127 Every term can be computed by a finite search: since a(n) <= n (see the Example section), only sets of at most n types need to be examined, and up to renaming the colors there are finitely many of them. Also a(n) tends to infinity, because for each t the finitely many sets of at most t types stop at only finitely many sizes. But there is no algorithm that, given t, returns a number N such that a(n) > t for all n > N: with such an N, a set of at most t types would tile the plane if and only if it tiles the (N+1) X (N+1) square, and by Berger's theorem no algorithm decides whether a given finite set of Wang tiles tiles the plane. In particular a(n) < g(n) for infinitely many n, for every computable, nondecreasing, unbounded function g.
%C A402127 For t >= 1, let B(t) be the largest n such that some set of at most t types stops at n; so B(t) is the largest n with a(n) <= t, and B(1..7) = 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 16. This function is an analog of the busy beaver function (A060843). It is called BB_WT on the BusyBeaverWiki, where the values B(5) = 6, B(6) = 8 and B(7) = 16 are credited to formal proofs by Yeyito (2026), checked by the proof assistant Rocq. A similar function, for disks instead of squares, was proposed by Goucher (2012), and the function f of Jeandel and Rao is B + 1. The largest size B(t) does not tell which smaller sizes are possible with at most t types: 13 is not possible with at most 7 types.
%C A402127 The bounds a(n) >= 7 for 9 <= n <= 16 follow from B(6) = 8, the bound a(13) >= 8 from an exhaustive search over all sets of seven types, and the bound a(17) >= 8 from Yeyito's theorem B(7) = 16; the opposite bounds come from explicit sets of tiles (see the Example and Links sections). The next term satisfies a(18) >= 8.
%C A402127 For n >= 3, the inequality a(n) > log(n)/(5*log(log(n))) (natural logarithms) holds for all n outside a set of density 0; compare the lower bound of the same form for the number of tile types that assemble an n X n square in the self-assembly model of Rothemund and Winfree. A similar question for Turing machines is A125269 (the least number of states of a two-symbol Turing machine that halts after exactly n steps on an initially blank tape). See the notes file in the Links section for proofs, sources and further data.
%H A402127 Robert Berger, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1090/memo/0066">The undecidability of the domino problem</a>, Memoirs Amer. Math. Soc. 66 (1966).
%H A402127 BusyBeaverWiki, <a href="https://wiki.bbchallenge.org/wiki/Wang_Tiles">Wang Tiles</a>.
