%H A402127 Alper Ferudun, <a href="/A402127/a402127_2.txt">Explicit sets of tiles for n = 1..17</a>.
%H A402127 Adam P. Goucher, <a href="https://cp4space.hatsya.com/2012/12/30/fast-growing-3/">Busy beavers</a>, Complex Projective 4-Space (blog), 2012.
%H A402127 Emmanuel Jeandel and Michaël Rao, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1506.06492">An aperiodic set of 11 Wang tiles</a>, arXiv:1506.06492 [cs.DM], 2015-2021; Adv. Comb. 2021:1, 37 pp.
%H A402127 Paul W. K. Rothemund and Erik Winfree, <a href="https://www.dna.caltech.edu/Papers/squares_STOC.pdf">The program-size complexity of self-assembled squares (extended abstract)</a>, Proc. 32nd ACM Symp. on Theory of Computing (STOC), 2000, 459-468.
%H A402127 Yeyito, <a href="https://github.com/Yeyito777/bbchallenge-discoveries/tree/302aa242b71910420f189dc4e342605448d03414/wang-tiles/rocq/wang">BB_WT in Rocq: BB_WT(5) = 6, BB_WT(6) = 8, BB_WT(7) = 16</a>, GitHub repository, 2026.
%F A402127 a(n) <= n.
%F A402127 a(n) <= 8*ceiling(log_2(n+1)).
%F A402127 a(n+1) <= 3*a(n) + 1 and a(n) <= a(n+1)^4.
%F A402127 a(n) < g(n) for infinitely many n, for every computable, nondecreasing, unbounded function g from the positive integers to the positive integers.
%F A402127 The number of n with 3 <= n <= x and a(n) <= log(n)/(5*log(log(n))) is o(x) as x -> oo.
%e A402127 Colors are written as integers, and a tile type is written (north, east, south, west).
%e A402127 n = 1: the single type A = (0, 1, 0, 0) covers one cell, but two copies cannot stand side by side (its east color is 1 and its west color is 0). So a(1) = 1.
%e A402127 n = 2: take A and B = (0, 2, 0, 1). The row A B can be put below itself, which tiles the 2 X 2 square, but no row has three cells, so this set stops at 2. One type alone cannot stop at 2: if it tiles the 2 X 2 square, it matches itself in both directions and tiles every square. So a(2) = 2.
%e A402127 In the same way the n types (0, i, 0, i-1), i = 1..n, stop at n, so a(n) <= n.
%e A402127 n = 6: five types suffice, namely A = (0, 0, 1, 0), B = (1, 1, 0, 0), C = (1, 2, 0, 1), D = (1, 3, 0, 2), E = (0, 3, 1, 3). The two rows
%e A402127   A A A B C D
%e A402127   B C D E E E
%e A402127 can be repeated alternately, which tiles the 6 X 6 square (see the picture in the Links section). In every tiling with these types a row consists of A's, then B, C, D, then E's (possibly cut off at either end), so a row contains at most three of the tiles B, C, D; and of two cells one above the other, exactly one holds B, C or D. So two rows, one above the other, have at most 3 + 3 = 6 cells each, and the 7 X 7 square cannot be tiled. Since B(4) = 4, no set of four types stops at 5 or 6, so a(5) = a(6) = 5.
%e A402127 Summary for n <= 17 (a set of a(n) types that stops at n is given for each n in the linked file):
%e A402127      n    | a(n) | fewer types are impossible because
%e A402127   --------+------+-----------------------------------------------
%e A402127    1..4   |  n   | B(n-1) = n-1
%e A402127    5, 6   |  5   | B(4) = 4
%e A402127    7, 8   |  6   | B(5) = 6
%e A402127    9..12  |  7   | B(6) = 8
%e A402127     13    |  8   | B(6) = 8, and no set of 7 types stops at 13
%e A402127   14..16  |  7   | B(6) = 8
%e A402127     17    |  8   | B(7) = 16
